WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:06.134
"Hurry up, Ned!" I shouted as stones and arrows continued to rain down on us while we fled.

2
00:00:06.135 --> 00:00:13.967
Ned couldn't stand to leave his provisions behind, so he tucked the hog and kangaroos under his arms and ran as fast as he could.

3
00:00:13.968 --> 00:00:19.126
In an instant the three of us loaded the boat, pushed it out to sea, and started rowing.

4
00:00:19.127 --> 00:00:24.864
"That was close," said Ned, mopping his brow. "But at least they didn't get our food."

5
00:00:24.865 --> 00:00:33.197
We hadn't gone more than five hundred feet when a hundred natives appeared on the shore, yelling and gesturing in a hostile manner.

6
00:00:33.198 --> 00:00:36.023
They rushed into the water up to their waists.

7
00:00:36.024 --> 00:00:42.189
I watched to see if the noise would draw some men from the Nautilus to the platform. But no.

8
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:46.261
The enormous submarine lying offshore appeared completely deserted.

9
00:00:46.262 --> 00:00:53.848
Twenty minutes later we were onboard the Nautilus again. I rushed to the gallery, where Captain Nemo was playing the organ.

10
00:00:53.849 --> 00:00:57.436
"Captain!" I said, but he didn't hear me.

11
00:00:57.437 --> 00:00:59.261
I touched his hand.

12
00:00:59.262 --> 00:01:04.840
He started and turned around. "Ah, Professor! Did you have a good hunt?"

13
00:01:04.841 --> 00:01:09.571
"Yes, but I'm afraid we've been pursued by some hostile natives."

14
00:01:09.572 --> 00:01:17.325
"Really?" Captain Nemo turned back to his keyboard, more interested in that than in my story. "How many do you think were after you?"

15
00:01:17.326 --> 00:01:19.087
"At least a hundred."

16
00:01:19.088 --> 00:01:22.823
The captain began to play again. "I wouldn't worry, Professor.

17
00:01:22.824 --> 00:01:28.309
All the natives of New Guinea could assemble on this shore, and the Nautilus would have nothing to fear."

18
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:33.666
The captain's confidence persuaded me, and I went to bed that night without any dread of the natives.

19
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:39.491
The sub's hatches were open, so the natives could have easily slipped into the interior of the submarine.

20
00:01:39.492 --> 00:01:44.801
But they must have been frightened by the sight of the long, dark monster aground in their bay. At

21
00:01:44.802 --> 00:01:48.626
six o'clock the next morning, I returned to the platform. The

22
00:01:48.627 --> 00:01:52.960
natives had taken advantage of low tide to come out on the coral. There

23
00:01:52.961 --> 00:01:57.698
must have been five or six hundred of them within several hundred feet of the sub. Nearly

24
00:01:57.699 --> 00:02:05.047
all of them were armed with bows, arrows, and shields, and they carried nets over their shoulders containing stones. The

25
00:02:05.048 --> 00:02:11.627
women wore grass skirts while some of the chiefs wore necklaces of red and white glass beads. One

26
00:02:11.628 --> 00:02:14.865
chief examined the Nautilus attentively. He

27
00:02:14.866 --> 00:02:22.023
was probably a man of high rank because he was draped in a mat of banana leaves set off with brilliant colors. During

28
00:02:22.024 --> 00:02:27.444
low tide the natives roamed near the Nautilus , but they weren't troublesome.

29
00:02:27.445 --> 00:02:35.356
They frequently gestured to me and repeated a phrase as if they were inviting me to go onshore. I declined to do so.

30
00:02:35.357 --> 00:02:43.515
When the tide rose at eleven o'clock, they went back on land. I noticed that now there were even more of them on the island.

31
00:02:43.516 --> 00:02:47.325
Having nothing to do, I called for Conseil to bring me a net.

32
00:02:47.326 --> 00:02:52.356
We spent the next two hours dragging for shells but found nothing unusual.

33
00:02:52.357 --> 00:02:57.832
But then when I least expected it, I put my hand on a rare specimen.

34
00:02:57.833 --> 00:03:01.928
"Conseil, look at this!" I cried, holding up the shell.

35
00:03:01.929 --> 00:03:08.817
"Oh, you have found something special indeed!" replied Conseil as he took the shell from my hand.

36
00:03:08.818 --> 00:03:15.984
Conseil and I were absorbed in studying the valuable shell when a stone broke the precious object in Conseil's hand.

37
00:03:15.985 --> 00:03:23.047
I uttered a cry of despair while Conseil picked up his gun and fired at a native who was still holding a slingshot.

38
00:03:23.048 --> 00:03:27.650
Conseil's shot broke a bracelet of amulets encircling the man's arm.

39
00:03:27.651 --> 00:03:35.063
"Conseil!" I cried. "No shell—not even a valuable one—is worth a man's life!"

40
00:03:35.064 --> 00:03:38.563
"The scoundrel!" snapped Conseil.

41
00:03:38.564 --> 00:03:42.706
"I wish he'd shattered my shoulder instead. Let's start the attack."

42
00:03:42.707 --> 00:03:50.523
I strongly disagreed. We were still discussing the matter when I looked up and saw that twenty canoes had surrounded the sub.

43
00:03:50.524 --> 00:03:56.047
Suddenly a shower of arrows hit the sub, and Conseil and I fled down the hatch.

44
00:03:56.048 --> 00:04:01.182
I went to the gallery but found no one there, so I knocked on the door of the captain's cabin.

45
00:04:01.183 --> 00:04:03.095
"Come in," he said.

46
00:04:03.096 --> 00:04:09.095
I entered and found Captain Nemo in the midst of some complicated mathematical calculations.

47
00:04:09.096 --> 00:04:12.460
"I'm disturbing you," I said politely.

48
00:04:12.461 --> 00:04:19.270
"That is true, Professor," Captain Nemo replied, "but you must have a serious reason for doing so."

49
00:04:19.271 --> 00:04:26.174
"Yes, sir. The natives have surrounded us with their canoes and in a few minutes they will certainly attack us!"

50
00:04:26.175 --> 00:04:32.849
"Well, then we must close all the hatches." Captain Nemo pressed a button to transmit an order to the crew.

51
00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:38.325
"You don't think their arrows could have any effect on the Nautilus, do you, Professor?" he asked me.

52
00:04:38.326 --> 00:04:42.571
"Remember, cannonballs from the Abraham Lincoln failed to pierce it."

53
00:04:42.572 --> 00:04:49.650
"No, sir. But I worry that the natives will get into the sub tomorrow morning when you open the hatches to take in fresh air."

54
00:04:49.651 --> 00:04:57.983
"Let them come in. I see no reason to stop them, and I don't want my visit here to cost the life of even one native," said Captain Nemo.

55
00:04:57.984 --> 00:05:03.309
"By the way, Professor, we'll be departing tomorrow afternoon at exactly 2:40."

56
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:08.134
He bowed and I knew he was dismissing me, so I returned to my cabin.

57
00:05:08.135 --> 00:05:16.126
That night I had trouble sleeping because the natives were stomping on the platform and uttering deafening cries.

58
00:05:16.127 --> 00:05:20.967
When I woke up the next morning, I could tell that the air had been renewed. The

59
00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:28.555
natives were still up on the platform, so Captain Nemo must have released some air from the reservoirs instead of opening the hatches. I

60
00:05:28.556 --> 00:05:35.523
worked in my room all morning without seeing Captain Nemo or seeing any evidence that we would be departing. At

61
00:05:35.524 --> 00:05:41.634
2:30 I went into the gallery; in only ten more minutes it would be high tide. If

62
00:05:41.635 --> 00:05:50.214
Captain Nemo were right, the Nautilus would soon be afloat once more; if not, we would be stuck here for who knows how long. I

63
00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:54.777
felt some vibrations as the ship's keel grated against the coral. At

64
00:05:54.778 --> 00:05:58.920
2:35 Captain Nemo entered the gallery. "We're

65
00:05:58.921 --> 00:06:04.103
going to start," he said. "I've given the order to open the hatches." "And

66
00:06:04.104 --> 00:06:06.444
the natives?" I said. "What

67
00:06:06.445 --> 00:06:08.508
about them?" "Won't

68
00:06:08.509 --> 00:06:12.151
they climb inside the Nautilus once the hatches are open?" "No.

69
00:06:12.152 --> 00:06:15.738
Follow me and see what happens." Captain

70
00:06:15.739 --> 00:06:19.008
Nemo led the way to the central staircase. Ned

71
00:06:19.009 --> 00:06:24.397
and Conseil were there, watching as the crew members opened the hatch to the platform. Twenty

72
00:06:24.398 --> 00:06:35.167
fierce faces peered down at us. But the first native who placed his hand on the stair rail jumped back, screaming and holding his hand in pain. Ned

73
00:06:35.168 --> 00:06:41.730
rushed onto the staircase and the moment he seized the railing with both hands, he fell to the ground. "I've

74
00:06:41.731 --> 00:06:45.064
been struck by lightning!" he cried. Now

75
00:06:45.065 --> 00:06:53.635
I understood what was going on. The railings were electrified wires, so anyone who touched them got a terrible shock. The

76
00:06:53.636 --> 00:07:03.547
natives made a hasty retreat; we went to Ned's aid; and Nautilus , lifted by the tide at exactly 2:40, moved away from the coral bed.

77
00:07:03.548 --> 00:07:11.286
Our speed increased, and we finally passed safely out of the dangerous Torres Strait.

